everyone knows that apple was founded by  the two steves steve jobs and steve  wozniak steve jobs was the inspiration  and marketing side of the business while  steve wozniak was the brains behind the  business but what about apple's third  co-founder ronald wayne ronald wayne was  the adult of the business and made sure  that the steves didn't go too far off  the edge yet most people myself included  had no clue that ronald was even  involved in the creation of apple and  that makes sense as ronald left apple  quite early on so he wasn't there when  apple finally made it ronald is still  alive today though so what happened to  apple's unknown co-founder  taking a look back ronald was born on  may 17 1934 in cleveland ohio  we don't know much about his early life  but it seems like ronald was quite  passionate about engineering design and  product development he ended up  attending the school of industrial arts  in new york city and after graduation in  1953 ronald taught himself  electromechanical engineering at the  time electrical and computer engineering  were still very much in the early stages  so ronald's self-education was more than  enough to get him a job in the field  in 1956 ronald moved to california where  he secured a job as chief engineer at a  military electronics company where he  would work for 16 years  near the end of his career at this  company ronald became a bit  entrepreneurial and decided to start his  own business in 1971 at an age of 37  ronald started selling sloth machines  unfortunately this was a massive failure  and ronald ended up going into  significant debt ronald later recalled  that he discovered very quickly that he  had no business going into business he  was a great engineer and it was best to  just call it at that so ronald picked up  a new engineering job at a new startup  called atari and he would spend the next  year repaying the debt he had  accumulated  just as ronald thought he was completely  done with business though he would run  into two co-workers at atari named steve  jobs and steve wozniak wozniak says that  he admired ronald's experience with  starting a business and that he looked  up to ronald for advice on life ronald  was substantially older than both  steve's so he easily molded to becoming  a sort of mentor in the duo's lives  ronald often settled debates between the  two and one of these debates would  actually turn into apple  in march of 1976 steve jobs came to  ronald with the conundrum he and wozniak  had been working on developing circuitry  for a personal computer jobs wanted to  leverage the circuitry and build a  personal computer business and wozniak  was on board with this idea  however wozniak did not understand why  they couldn't just sell the circuitry  itself as well  jobs tried to explain that the circuitry  should be proprietary and that selling  it would just put them at a disadvantage  but wozniak didn't quite understand so  jobs wanted ronald to explain to wozniak  why he shouldn't sell the circuitry  ronald agreed and told the two to come  to his apartment on april 1 1976 to sort  out the issue when ronald explained the  situation to wozniak wozniak quickly  came around and agreed to not sell the  circuitry jobs was quite impressed with  how easily ronald was able to convince  wozniak and both the jobs and wozniak  looked up to ronald so jobs figured it  would be a good idea to get ronald to  join the company as well right after  ronald convinced wozniack jobs asked  ronald if he would be willing to join  apple ronald agreed and the trio would  make it official by incorporating the  company both steves owned 45 each while  ronald owned 10  this way ronald was able to break ties  between the steves after founding the  company ronald would get to work on  illustrating their first apple logo  writing the apple one manual and  drafting the trio's partnership  agreement while ronald worked on these  administrative tasks jobs went out and  started making aggressive deals which  made ronald quite uncomfortable  for example jobs took out a 15 000 line  of credit to buy materials required to  fill apple's first order that's the same  as a 72 000 line of credit today  to make things even worse apple's first  customer was a company called the bike  shop which was notorious for paying out  its vendors late and here's the thing  everyone involved in the partnership was  personally responsible for paying back  the debt now ideally wozniak and jobs  would be responsible for 90 of the debt  and ronald would only be responsible for  10 of the debt but the problem was that  wozniak and jobs were broke 20 year olds  with no assets so if things turned sour  wozniak and jobs would likely just file  for bankruptcy and it would be up to  ronald to pay back the creditors in  other words if their business did well  ronald only got 10 percent of their  profit but if the business did poorly  ronald will experience 100 of the  fallback given that ronald had just  recently spent a year digging himself  out of debt ronald wasn't willing to  take such a massive financial risk  something else that dissuaded ronald  from sticking around was the type of  work he had to do  as we previously discussed ronald's  passion was engineering and product  design but at apple ronald mainly just  worked on administrative tasks while  wosniak and jobs actually built the core  of the business ronald didn't feel like  it was his place to ask for a  product-oriented role in the company  either as he recognized that he was  quote standing in the shadow of giants  so 12 days after joining the company  ronald would respectfully part ways with  wozniak and jobs and renounce his role  in the company in return for his 10  percent stake in apple ronald received a  total of 800  or about 3 800 today wozniak actually  disagrees with this timeline claiming  that ronald didn't leave until a few  months after the company was started  either way though the consensus is that  ronald left very early on well before  apple found any sort of substantial  success  ronald stayed at atari up until 1978 at  which point he switched lawrence  livermore national laboratory meanwhile  jobs wasn't very fond of ronald's  decision and he would attempt to get  ronald back on board for quite some time  about a year after ronald's departure  though jobs finally gave in and moved  ensuring that there were no lingering  remains of ronald in the business jobs  paid ronald an additional 1500 and got  him to sign a quick claim deed which  basically meant that ronald gave up all  legal rights to apple or any of its  assets and with that it was official  ronald was completely out of the  business for a total of 2 300  or about 11 000 today later on in the  1970s ronald would start his own  business but it was nowhere near as  ambitious as apple  ronald opened a small stamp shop in  california but unfortunately he would  experience a number of robberies and  ronald would decide to move his business  across the border to nevada  jobs would approach ronald one more time  with a request to connect jobs with one  of ronald's friends  jobs was looking to buy the company that  ronald's friend owned but ronald refused  to relay the message  ronald felt that his friend should  retain control of his company and that  his friends should form an exclusive  licensing deal with apple as opposed to  selling out altogether  ronald later regretted this decision  citing that it was not his decision to  make  and that was the last time jobs tried to  contact ronald for a business  anyway ronald did take a few remnants  with him to nevada from his days at  apple ronald used a very similar logo  for his damn shop wayne's philatelics  ronald also still had the original apple  partnership contract paper which he  would sell in the early 1990s for 500  that same contract paper would end up  auctioning for 1.6 million dollars in  2011 and ronald did end up regretting  the sale as there was really no  financial risk to just holding on to the  contract and that brings us into the  billion dollar question did ronald  regret his decision to leave apple and  was he salty for giving up his stake  well the answer is no  i'm sure ronald wishes he stayed around  but he does not regret his decision to  leave ronald says that he made the best  decision he could with the information  he had at the time  moreover he didn't leave apple because  he thought the company would fail  in fact he thought the company would  succeed that's why he joined the company  but he knew that the road to success was  going to be extremely bumpy which he was  right about and he simply wasn't willing  to experience the ups and downs of the  journey ronald describes that leading  jobs and wozniak was like having a tiger  by its tail and that he was getting old  and couldn't keep up with that ronald  claims that the stress of staying at  apple would have likely resulted in him  becoming the richest man in the cemetery  so it wasn't like ronald was waiting for  apple to fail after he left or anything  in fact i think he's quite happy about  apple's success  considering this extremely mature  approach to missing out on one of the  biggest opportunities in history it's no  wonder why the steves wanted ronald  around  but what if ronald held on to a stake  how much would he be worth today  well apple currently stands at a  valuation of 2.4 trillion dollars  meaning that the entire 10 percent stake  would be worth 240 billion dollars but  it's completely unrealistic to expect  ronald to hold on to an entire 10  percent for 45 years  the best case scenario would be ronald  selling two percent for living expenses  and retaining an eight percent stake in  the company which would give him a net  worth of 192 billion dollars more  realistically though ronald would likely  hold a three to five percent stake today  which comes out to about 100 billion  dollars something else to consider is  that apple nearly went bankrupt in the  late 1990s so i don't think ronald would  have held it through that uncertainty  considering this ronald would have  likely down to stake in the 80s and 90s  even if he stuck around at apple meaning  that he would have cashed out for a few  hundred million dollars which is a far  cry from 100 billion dollars in any case  though sticking around at apple would  have resulted in a much better life for  ronald financially as ronald lives a  pretty modest retirement life today he  currently lives in a mobile home park in  nevada and makes some money on the side  selling stamps and rare coins and  playing penny slots in vegas  that's quite a stark contrast to what  his life could have been but i think  ronald says it best  what can i say you make a decision based  on your understanding of the  circumstances and you live with it would  you guys have left apple if you were in  ronald's situation comment that down  below  also drop a like if you respect how  ronald handled the missed opportunity  and of course consider joining our  discord community to suggest future  video ideas and consider subscribing to  see more questions ultimately answered  but until then i'm hari and i'll see you  guys on the next one  [Music]  you
 

